   "l": "Push emptiness to the utmost;\nguard stillness, and firmly.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Carry emptiness as far as it will go; hold stillness fast.",
   "n": "Two lines of three characters, built the same way: verb, object, and then a word that describes the doing rather than the thing — 極 'to the last degree', 篤 'solidly'. Nothing marks them as adverbs; Chinese has no such marking, and position does all the work. One of the two Mawangdui manuscripts writes 督 where ours has 篤 (the other reads differently again). It is usually explained as another way of writing the same word, but that is a conjecture: 督 has a sense of its own, the middle seam that runs down a thing.",
   "p": true
