   "l": "Now the things, rank and teeming,\neach returns again to its root.\nTo return to the root is called stillness;\nthis is called returning to what was allotted.\nTo return to what was allotted is called the abiding;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Things grow rank and thick, and each goes back again to its own root. Going back to the root is called stillness; this is what is meant by returning to what was allotted. To return to what was allotted is the abiding thing;",
   "n": "復 does two different jobs within one line of each other. In 各復歸其根 it is the adverb 'again'; in 復命 it is the verb 'return to'. Nothing on the page separates them. 芸芸 is a doubled descriptive like 緜緜 in chapter 6 — the repetition is the meaning, growth on growth. 命 is not fate in the sense of doom: it is what heaven has handed out to a thing, its allotted span and portion, and to return to it is to go back to what one was given rather than what one has made. Then a chain of definitions, A 曰 B, B 曰 C, each link picking up the last — a way of arguing you will meet again three lines down."
  },
