   "l": "To know the abiding is called seeing clear.\nNot knowing the abiding —\nreckless acting: disaster.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "To know what abides is to see clearly. Not knowing it, a man acts at random, and it ends badly.",
   "n": "妄作凶 is three characters and two thoughts with nothing between them: 'act recklessly' and 'disaster'. The 'and so' is the reader's to supply — this language leaves the joints of an argument open far more often than English can bear, and the chapter's last six lines will do the same thing six times over. 明 was two adjectives in chapter 10's 明白四達; here it is the noun, the clarity that comes of knowing how things go round."
